[META]
framework: claude-framework
file_id: workflow.GATES
class: universal
version: 1.0.0

[GATES]
overview: Confirmation gates — points in the workflow where the agent MUST stop and request explicit user approval
principle: A gate is a checkpoint, not a suggestion. Silence is not approval. "Sounds reasonable" is not approval. Only explicit "yes / proceed / go ahead" passes the gate.

[HARD_GATE_1_PLAN_APPROVAL]
location: End of PHASES Phase 3 (CONFIRM)
trigger: ALWAYS — every non-trivial task has this gate (see ESCAPE_HATCH.toon for the few exceptions)
what_to_ask: Present the full plan, then ask: "Proceed with this plan? [yes / amend / reject]"
waiting_behavior:
  - Do NOT start implementation while waiting
  - Do NOT "prepare" files in anticipation
  - Do NOT read more files "to be ready"
  - Answer any follow-up questions, then re-ask
what_counts_as_approval:
  - "yes" / "go" / "proceed" / "approved" / "LGTM" / "ok let's do it"
what_does_NOT_count:
  - Silence
  - "Sounds good" followed by concerns
  - "I think so"
  - "Maybe"
  - "Whatever you think is best" — this is the user deferring, not approving; push back and ask them to decide

[HARD_GATE_2_PUBLIC_API_SHAPE]
location: During Phase 3, if the plan introduces or changes a public API
trigger: New exported function / class / HTTP route / CLI flag / config key / database schema / event shape
what_to_ask: Present the API shape as a separate, focused question: "Here is the API signature: <signature>. Is this correct? [yes / revise]"
why: Public APIs are harder to change later than internal code. Getting explicit sign-off on the shape prevents expensive reversals.

[HARD_GATE_3_DESTRUCTIVE_OR_WIDE_BLAST_RADIUS]
location: During Phase 3, if the plan includes ANY of the following
triggers:
  - Deleting files or directories
  - Deleting/dropping database columns, tables, indexes
  - Force-pushing, rewriting git history, rebasing shared branches
  - Modifying CI/CD pipelines
  - Adding or removing dependencies (new `npm install`, `pip install`, new submodule)
  - Changing permissions / roles / secrets
  - Running migrations on user data
  - Renaming files or symbols (see REFACTOR.toon RULE_6)
  - Mass find-and-replace across >5 files
what_to_ask: Explicitly call out the destructive/wide action as its own question. "This plan will <action>. Confirm explicitly: <yes / no>"
special_rule: Get confirmation PER destructive action if the plan contains multiple

[HARD_GATE_4_MID_IMPLEMENTATION_DEVIATION]
location: Inside Phase 4 (IMPLEMENT) when reality diverges from the plan
trigger: ANY of the following discovered mid-implementation
  - A constraint the plan missed
  - An error the plan didn't anticipate
  - A file that needs changes beyond what the plan listed
  - A rename / deletion / new dependency not in the plan
  - A realization that the approach won't work
what_to_do:
  - STOP immediately. Do not push through.
  - Return to PLAN phase with the new information
  - Re-run CONFIRM gate with the amended plan
why: "I'll adjust as I go" is how approved plans silently become unapproved ones

[HARD_GATE_5_BEFORE_IRREVERSIBLE_ACTION]
location: Just before executing an irreversible action, even if previously approved in the plan
trigger: The specific action is about to run (not just approved in principle)
examples:
  - About to run `git push --force`
  - About to run a production migration
  - About to delete files that aren't tracked by git
  - About to send a message / open a PR / publish anything externally visible
what_to_do: Re-confirm at the point of execution, even if the plan was approved. "About to <action> — confirm? [yes / abort]"
rationale: Plan approval is conditional; the final trigger pull is its own decision

[SOFT_GATE_1_UNKNOWN_CONVENTION]
location: In DISCOVER or PLAN when you can't determine the project's convention from evidence
trigger: No existing similar pattern found, or conflicting patterns exist
what_to_do: Ask the user rather than guess. "I don't see an existing pattern for X. Prefer A or B?"
why: Guessing produces code that doesn't match the project's style; a 5-second question prevents a 30-minute rework

[SOFT_GATE_2_AMBIGUOUS_REQUEST]
location: In DISCOVER when the user's request has plausible multiple interpretations
trigger: Task could mean 2+ different things, and the wrong interpretation would waste significant work
what_to_do: State the interpretations back and ask. "I read this as either A or B. Which?"
why: Cheaper to ask than to rewrite

[GATE_CHECKLIST_BEFORE_CODING]
Every time you're about to write production code, answer:
  - [ ] Is there an approved plan covering exactly this change?
  - [ ] Did the user explicitly approve, not just acknowledge?
  - [ ] Are there any hard-gate triggers I haven't cleared?
  - [ ] If this is an ESCAPE_HATCH case, does it truly match the criteria (not a rationalization)?

If ANY answer is "no" or "not sure" → do NOT write code. Return to the appropriate phase or gate.
